WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.080
Safe implementation begins away from your primary system.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:08.180
Use a virtual machine, spare disk, lab laptop, external drive,

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.560
or other recoverable test environment.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:16.300
RAM-backed boot designs often touch early boot, root filesystem layout, mounts,

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:17.960
and persistence.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.820
A small mistake can leave a system unable to boot or can make changes disappear

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.560
unexpectedly. Testing in a lab keeps learning reversible.

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:31.340
Before changing a real system, back up important data and configuration.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:36.920
That includes bootloader configuration, initramfs configuration, fstab, crypttab,

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.780
recovery keys, important files,

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.920
and any distribution-specific configuration that affects root mounting.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:48.620
Keep recovery media available. Keep a known-good boot entry so you can return to a working

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:49.640
path.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:56.020
If disk encryption, secure boot, vendor firmware, hybrid graphics, Wi-Fi firmware,

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.640
or special storage drivers are involved, treat them as compatibility variables.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:04.860
Troubleshooting should be methodical. Check the kernel command line that actually booted.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.240
Verify which initramfs was loaded.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:12.740
Read boot logs and journal output when available. Confirm the root filesystem source.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.480
Inspect mount points.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:20.380
Check which layer is writable and where the upper layer lives. Confirm whether tmpfs size

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:21.800
limits are set.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:26.740
Compare expected persistence with what actually survived reboot. Common failures come from

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:27.920
assumptions.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:33.580
A driver needed for storage may be missing from initramfs. A root path may be wrong. A

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.520
boot parameter may not be supported by the distribution.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:41.220
The system may not have enough RAM for the root image plus workload. A persistent mount

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:43.040
may not appear early enough.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:47.900
A laptop may behave differently from a virtual machine because of firmware or graphics.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:53.400
Do not troubleshoot by randomly editing boot files on a primary machine. Change one thing

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:54.660
at a time,

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.520
keep notes, and preserve a working fallback.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:01.180
A safe RAM-boot project is not just a clever boot line.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:05.800
It is a tested design with recovery media, backups, known-good boot entries,

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.100
and documentation.
